 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed board changes are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. This provides low-latency updates without recurring realtime vendor fees or provider coupling, accepting the operational and design work of managing WebSocket connection capacity, replayable revision storage, and browser reconnect/catch-up. Five-second polling was rejected for its repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. See [Change Delivery Mechanism](decisions/change-delivery.md).
